# Autoloading Classes in PHPUnit Using Composer and autoload.php

When you install PHPUnit via Composer, you get more than just the testing framework; you receive a sophisticated autoloader designed to resolve class names without manual `include` or `require` statements. However, getting your own application classes picked up by this system requires a specific configuration step that is often overlooked in older tutorials. The core issue lies in understanding where Composer places its generated files and how it discovers which of those files should be loaded.

In your directory structure, the file `vendor/autoload.php` acts as the central hub for all autoloading logic. This file contains the bootstrap code necessary to map class names to their corresponding file paths. When you run `./vendor/bin/phpunit`, PHPUnit executes this file at the start of every test suite execution. If your application classes reside in a directory like `lib/` but are not registered with Composer, PHPUnit will naturally fail with a "Class not found" error because it cannot locate them using standard path resolution or PSR-0 conventions without explicit configuration.

To fix this, you must modify the `composer.json` file to register your application's source code as part of the autoload map. This is achieved by adding an entry to the `autoload` key within the file. You should define a namespace that matches your directory structure and point it to the physical location of your classes.

For example, if you want to load classes from the `lib/` directory without forcing them into a strict namespaced hierarchy, you can use the `psr-0` autoloading standard. This allows you to define a virtual namespace (e.g., `MyApp`) that maps directly to your `lib` folder.

```json
{
    "autoload": {
        "psr-0": {
            "MyApp": "lib/"
        }
    },
    "require": {
        "phpunit/phpunit": "3.7.*",
        "phpunit/phpunit-selenium": ">=1.2"
    }
}
```

Once you have added this configuration, you must regenerate the `vendor/autoload.php` file. You can do this by running `composer dump-autoload`. This command updates the autoloader with your new mappings so that PHPUnit can find classes like `Returning` when they are referenced in `returningTest.php`.

It is important to note that simply having `autoload.php` in the `vendor/` directory does not automatically load your application code. The file only knows about dependencies listed under the `require` or `require-dev` keys unless you explicitly tell it to look in your own directories via the `autoload` configuration. Without this step, PHPUnit treats your project files as if they need manual inclusion.

When working with namespaces and PSR-0 standards, consistency is key. If you choose to use the `namespace` keyword inside your PHP class files (e.g., `namespace MyApp;`), ensure that the path defined in `composer.json` reflects that structure accurately. For instance, if your file is at `lib/Returning.php`, and you want to use the namespace `MyApp\Returning`, the mapping must be precise.

By configuring the autoloader correctly, you eliminate the need for tedious manual includes in every test file. This approach keeps your test code clean and ensures that changes to your class structure are automatically reflected during testing without requiring additional configuration updates. While modern PHP projects often use PSR-4 autoloading exclusively, understanding how to configure `psr-0` remains essential when maintaining legacy codebases or working with older PHPUnit versions like 3.7.x.

For those looking for a streamlined setup that handles these configurations automatically, the community has developed various project skeletons. One notable example is the [PHPUnit Skeleton](https://github.com/jasdeepkhalsa/phpunit-skeleton), which provides a ready-made directory structure and configuration files to get testing up and running quickly without needing to manually manage the `composer.json` autoloader settings from scratch.
